# Git and Production safety

Follow these gates for every conversation. **Explicit approval** means the user
clearly authorizes that **specific** operation in the current request.

Phrases like “implement this task” or “finish the feature” do **not** authorize
merge, direct push to `main`, Production deploy, force-push, history rewrite,
or destructive cloud/database work.

## Required approval before acting

| Operation | Rule |
| --------- | ---- |
| Push to `main` | Never without explicit approval. Prefer feature branches and PRs. Direct push to `main` is allowed when the user explicitly asks for it. |
| Merge a PR | Never without explicit approval. |
| Deploy to Production | Never without explicit approval. Merging to `main` deploys Production — treat merge as deploy only when the user explicitly approves merge (or deploy). |
| Force-push, reset shared branches, rewrite history | Never without explicit approval. |
| Production DB migrations, destructive Neon/Vercel/cloud changes | Never without explicit approval. |

## Allowed without extra gates

Creating branches, implementing tasks, running tests, committing on a feature
branch, pushing that branch, and opening a PR when the work is ready for review.
These are normal agent workflow — still do **not** merge, push to `main`
(unless explicitly approved), or deploy without the gates above.

When in doubt, stop and ask before push-to-`main`, merge, deploy, force-push,
or destructive production operations.

## Activated merge pipeline

After explicit one-time activation of the controlled migration/deployment
pipeline, maintainer approval to merge the reviewed schema change into staging,
or the reviewed release into main, authorizes that branch's documented migration
and deployment steps. Do not request a duplicate confirmation for those same
steps. Tests must pass on the exact merged commit before mutation. Preparing or
updating a PR does not authorize activating the pipeline, changing hosted
credentials/settings, running a migration, merging, or deploying. Seed, reset,
restore, destructive maintenance, npm publication and tag/release publication
remain separate operations. See [database rollout](../../docs/migrations.md).
